Package: lagpower
Type: Package
Title: Genetic Lag Time and Power to Detect Population Differentiation
Version: 0.0.1
Authors@R: c(
    person("Yacine", "Ben Chehida", email = "yacine.benchehida@nhm.ac.uk",
           role = c("aut", "cre")),
    person("Anna", "Schleimer", role = "aut"),
    person("Xenia", "Moreira Lopez", role = "aut"))
Description: Simulates the split of one ancestral population into two daughter
    populations, with genetic drift, gene flow and mutation, and estimates how
    many generations must pass before the split becomes statistically
    detectable with a given set of markers and samples (the "grey zone" of
    differentiation; Bailleul et al. 2018 <doi:10.1111/eva.12591>; Ben Chehida
    et al. 2020 <doi:10.1038/s41437-019-0284-1>; Schleimer et al. 2024
    <doi:10.1111/icad.12725>). It helps decide whether an absence of genetic
    structure reflects a genetic lag-time effect, a lack of statistical power,
    or genuine connectivity. The simulation engine is written in C++ (Rcpp)
    and follows allele frequencies, so very large effective sizes are cheap.
